. What is RAG?
• Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) combines information retrieval with text generation.
• A RAG system retrieves relevant information from an external knowledge source and provides that information
to a language model before generating an answer.
6. Why Use RAG?
• RAG can help applications answer questions using private, domain-specific, or frequently updated information.
• Examples include question answering over company documents, university notes, manuals, research papers,
and project documentation.
7. Main RAG Pipeline
• 1. Load data from sources such as TXT, CSV, PDF, or web pages.
• 2. Split large documents into smaller chunks.
• 3. Convert chunks into numerical embeddings.
• 4. Store embeddings in a vector database or vector store.
• 5. Retrieve the most relevant chunks for a user question.
• 6. Give the question and retrieved context to an LLM to generate the answer.
8. RAG Components
• Document Loader: Loads information from files or other sources.
• Text Splitter: Divides documents into smaller chunks.
• Embedding Model: Converts text into vectors representing semantic meaning.
• Vector Store: Stores and searches vector representations.
• Retriever: Finds relevant documents or chunks.
• LLM: Generates a natural-language response using the retrieved context.
9. Python + RAG
• Python is commonly used for RAG because its ecosystem provides tools for document processing,
embeddings, vector stores, and language models.
• A typical project may use LangChain for orchestration, an embedding model for vectors, Chroma as a vector
store, and an LLM for response generation.
10. Example RAG Flow
• A user asks: What is LangChain?
• The retriever searches the knowledge base for relevant LangChain information.
• The retrieved chunks are provided to the language model with the question.
• The model generates an answer using the retrieved context.
11. Important RAG Terms
• Chunk: A smaller section of a document.
• Embedding: A numerical representation of text meaning.
• Vector: A list of numbers representing information mathematically.
• Similarity Search: Finds vectors that are close to a query vector.
• Context: Retrieved information supplied to the language model.
• Hallucination: An incorrect or unsupported model-generated statement.
12. Simple Python + RAG Structure
• A project can separate document loading, chunking, embeddings, retrieval, and generation into different
Python files.
• Example folders: data/, loaders/, embeddings/, vectorstore/, retrieval/, and main.py.